From: cactus@bb.hks.net (L. Todd Masco)
Date: 29 Nov 1994 19:37:13 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-504] SCIENCE: Artificial Self-Replicators

This week's Science News (Nov 26, 1994, Vol. 146, No. 22) contains a 
cover story on "Artificial Self-Replicators."  All quotes are from that
issue.

It discusses in very broad, ambiguous terms, the work of Julius Rebek,
"a chemist at MIT."  

In essence, it distinguishes between two active areas of self-replicating
molecule research: biological and non-biological.  It claims that Rebek
is one of the few doing non-biological (non D/R-NA) systems, using organic
solvents (due to their relatively fast reaction speeds and ease of
observation).

Two families of Rebek's work are mentioned: the first was synthesized in
1990, (and refers back to Science News: 2/3/90, p.69). "Though the system
turned out to be slower and less efficient than they had hoped, the
molecules did copy themselves by way of complementarity."

The second family is reported on in the Oct. 5 _Journal_Of_The_American_
Chemical_Society_ (which I do not receive: should anybody have a copy
of the paper, I'd be glad to have a copy and maybe type it in.  I don't
think I'll be able to get to a library for this).  

"'We've improved the structure by tweaking some fine points,' says M.
 Morgan Conn, a chemist at [UCBerkeley].  The first generation of
 molecules showed that, for a template to reproduce itself, it must hold
 molecular pieces firmly enough in place so that they can bind together
 without wriggling around. 'Position is very important,' adds Conn. 'So
 we tighted up the molecule's receptors,' where self-assembly takes
 place."

The second generation also is improved by the blocking of undesired
reactions.

The rest of the article focuses on the links between life and self-
replicating structures.
