Subject: [#94-11-514] BASICS: Anarchy is not Chaos

At  8:57 PM 11/29/94 -0500, Phil Goetz replied to Mistress Romana:
>Most people, in the past and in the present, would not kill each other.
>There have always been some who would without restraint.

What keeps these few from killing under the current system?

I suspect you would answer: the police or the government.

Well, I'll grant you that the state [police & government as a whole] does
deter some few of these people from going on a killing spree. Most,
however, simply kill the state-disarmed victims and then, perhaps, get
killed or detained later by something passing for justice.

That same deterence could be provided as well or better by letting all the
potential victims arm themselves. If you add to that, mutual protection
societies, private security forces and polycentric law, you'll have an
anarchy that is not a "dog-eat-dog, red-in-tooth-and-claw jungle".
(Polycentric Law, aka - Privately Produced Law, check out the URL at
http://www.c2.org/~arkuat/faq/ppl.txt, and/or or read Friedman's "Machinery
of Freedom" for details.)

>Why don't you answer the question?

She is trying to get you to answer the question yourself, since that would
be far more valuable to you than her answer.

Or rather, in this case, rephrase the question into something more useful
and then answer it yourself.

